WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.080
This shouldn't surprise you at this point,&nbsp;
but Heather, Josh and the other stars of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.960
the "Blair Witch" films didn't really die. Some&nbsp;
launched careers in Hollywood, while others have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.960
turned their back on the business. Here's what&nbsp;
happened to the cast of the "Blair Witch" movies.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.480
She's the most recognizable face of&nbsp;
the "Blair Witch" franchise -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:22.480
that's not necessarily a good thing. As Heather&nbsp;
Donahue sadly admitted to The Guardian in 2016,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.160
"My name and face are for ever going to&nbsp;
be someone else’s intellectual property."

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.280
This is what happened when she shot&nbsp;
to stardom playing "herself" as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.720
film's lead Blair Witch believer&nbsp;
and documentarian investigator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.120
While she said that making the film was a&nbsp;
"joy," immediate success afterwards was elusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.960
especially as the marketing team at Artisan&nbsp;
Entertainment wanted her to act as though she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:47.520
were really dead. For a long time her IMDb profile&nbsp;
listed her as deceased at age 24. This confusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.920
about her mortality status didn't help land her&nbsp;
many subsequent roles, but she did manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.040
carve out parts in a dozen or so movies later,&nbsp;
before calling it quits on Hollywood in 2007.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.000
Her next move was to the Sierra Nevada foothills,&nbsp;
where she became a grower of medical marijuana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.480
Donahue's high times became the basis for her&nbsp;
green memoir, "Growgirl," which was published in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.160
2012. She then co-created and tried to shop around&nbsp;
a pot comedy pilot called "The High Country."

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.400
These days, Donahue is currently focused on&nbsp;
developing a line of herbal skin-care products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.600
and inspiring and teaching others to&nbsp;
share their own stories through writing.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.600
Josh Leonard told The Guardian back in 2018,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.440
"I was up for any kind of adventure back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.400
so said yes when [directors Daniel Myrick and&nbsp;
Eduardo Sánchez] asked me to play the cameraman."

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.480
Leonard also experienced troubles being&nbsp;
listed as deceased on IMDb by the Artisan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.320
marketing department, to the point that his&nbsp;
parents started getting condolence calls in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.040
response to the fake news. His career came very&nbsp;
much alive after the film, when he decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.360
earnestly pursue the craft of acting and stop,&nbsp;
quote, "being the guy from 'Blair Witch.'"

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.680
A year later he appeared opposite Robert De&nbsp;
Niro and Cuba Gooding Jr. in "Men of Honor,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.160
followed by a steady diet of independent films,&nbsp;
network TV shows, and video game voice overs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.200
Inspired by his work in Lynn Shelton's&nbsp;
improvised 2009 mumblecore film "Humpday,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.480
he branched out as a director,&nbsp;
with 2011's "The Lie," and later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.880
with 2018's "Dark Was The Night,"&nbsp;
and 2020's "Fully Realized Humans."

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.880
Leonard continues to be a working actor&nbsp;
in projects like Steven Soderbergh's 2018&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.440
iPhone horror flick "Unsane" and TV series&nbsp;
including "Bates Motel" and "Togetherness."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.880
He has been married to fellow&nbsp;
actor Allison Pill since 2015.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.000
Michael Williams is a very common name, but the&nbsp;
Bronx born sometimes-actor knows you know him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.120
better as "Mike" aka "The map guy" aka "The guy&nbsp;
in the corner" from "The Blair Witch Project."

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.160
He extended his resume by guesting on a few TV&nbsp;
shows like "Law &amp; Order" and "FBI," as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.200
reuniting with director Eduardo Sánchez for his&nbsp;
2006 horror film "Altered." Williams enlisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.400
Sánchez and his "Witch" co-star Joshua Leonard&nbsp;
for the 2013 webseries "Four Corners of Fear."

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.080
While he does have a few new projects on the&nbsp;
horizon, Williams has moved away from having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.040
full time career as an actor. He currently works&nbsp;
as a school counselor, is an adjunct faculty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.960
member at the Jacob Burns Media Arts Lab, and runs&nbsp;
the MCW Acting Studio alongside his wife Toni.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.600
Williams is currently writing&nbsp;
a book about his debut film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.640
with the working title "Escaping&nbsp;
the Most Famous Corner in Hollywood:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.040
How I Survived the Blair Witch." In his free&nbsp;
time, he enjoys fishing in the great outdoors.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.280
Ed Swanson isn't much of a fisherman,&nbsp;
nor an actor, but he played up both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.200
in "The Blair Witch Project," as our trio&nbsp;
of filmmakers headed deep into the woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.680
Swanson was on director Eduardo Sánchez's softball&nbsp;
team and was asked to play a part in the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.000
Besides that performance, Swanson hasn't&nbsp;
acted outside of community and church theater.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
He was in the Marine Corps, and got out right&nbsp;
before the Gulf War. This brought him to Maryland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.920
where the film was shot, and he still resides&nbsp;
there to this day. Now in his fifties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.440
he's carved out a niche for himself&nbsp;
as an artisan who creates clocks.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.760
As for his fishing buddy with the hat in the film,&nbsp;
Bob Griffin? He had never met him before the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.840
nor saw him after. According to Swanson,&nbsp;
his scene partner has since passed away.

00:03:48.954 --> 00:03:52.000
Susie Gooch was in the right place at the&nbsp;
right time when she and her young daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.760
Ingrid were interviewed for what they thought&nbsp;
was an actual student film. They didn't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.840
much about their ad-libbed lines until a friend&nbsp;
two years later said they'd seen them in a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.400
called "The Blair Witch Project," where Susie&nbsp;
was listed in the credits as "Jackie Hallex."

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.320
The non-acting, natural ladies relish their&nbsp;
roles in the film, and have happily appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.800
at screenings and filming location tours in the&nbsp;
years since. Susie has been a teacher for over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.280
34 years and currently works as a tutor&nbsp;
in Maryland. Ingrid graduated from Hood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.280
College with a Master's of Science in Clinical&nbsp;
Mental Health Counseling, is currently the Lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.080
Clinician at an Outpatient Recovery Centers, is&nbsp;
a big Korn fan, and is engaged to be married.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.200
Attempting to capitalize on the hoopla behind&nbsp;
"The Blair Witch Project," a quasi-sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.240
"Book of Shadows: Blair Witch&nbsp;
2," hit theaters a year later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.600
launching a filming locations "tour"&nbsp;
of the first film within this film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.440
led by Jeffery Patterson. Patterson was&nbsp;
played by Jeffrey Donovan, who knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.680
risks of alienating fans of the original&nbsp;
by outright saying it was pure fiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.000
Fans and critics didn't care for this approach,&nbsp;
but it had little effect on Donovan's career.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.480
His acting visibility started to pick up steam&nbsp;
when he appeared in the 2005 Will Smith vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.640
"Hitch," and two years later he landed a&nbsp;
stint on TV's "Crossing Jordan." Leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.280
man status was achieved when he got noticed as&nbsp;
Michael Westen in USA Network's "Burn Notice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.760
In between he played Robert F. Kennedy in Clint&nbsp;
Eastwood's "J. Edgar," and later his brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.120
John F. in Rob Reiner's "LBJ." Other works of&nbsp;
note include Season 2 of "Fargo," playing opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.920
Angelina Jolie in Eastwood's "Changeling," and&nbsp;
in "Lucy in the Sky" alongside Natalie Portman.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.640
When Kim Director heard about a "Blair&nbsp;
Witch" sequel she was skeptical at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.760
but "hit the floor" when she found out&nbsp;
she was going to be directed by acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.640
"Paradise Lost" documentarian Joe Berlinger. She&nbsp;
described her psychic Kim Diamond character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.880
"Book of Shadows" as a "very intuitive,&nbsp;
slightly depressed, acidic goth girl."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.920
Director feels the follow-up didn't&nbsp;
do well because of poor marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.880
and that it ultimately "had a lot&nbsp;
that it had to fight against."

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.560
"I thought the movie was cool."

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.560
Despite its failure, "Book of Shadows" was&nbsp;
a breakout role for Director — actually her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.960
forth one, after securing small parts in the&nbsp;
Spike Lee films "He Got Game," "Summer of Sam"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:56.560
and "Bamboozled." She reunited with Lee in&nbsp;
2004 for "She Hate Me," 2006's "Inside Man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.960
and the Netflix series version of "She's Gotta&nbsp;
Have it." Outside of her Lee collaborations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.720
she has kept busy with other films, as well&nbsp;
as appearances in notable shows like "Sex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.800
and the City," "Law &amp; Order," "Orange is the&nbsp;
New Black," "The Good Wife," and "Gotham."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.760
One of her larger, more memorable recent roles&nbsp;
was as the sex worker Shay on HBO's "The Deuce."

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.520
2016's "Blair Witch" was more of a true&nbsp;
sequel to the original than "Book of Shadows."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.320
It finds Heather's younger brother&nbsp;
James heading into those same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.000
scary woods to try and find his&nbsp;
sister. McCune told Digital Trends,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.880
"There was a lot of pressure to live&nbsp;
up to the standards of the first one."

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.440
The film literally gave him nightmares,&nbsp;
and in 2016 he told USA Today,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.600
"I had dreams of being chased by a creature down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.880
an alleyway on a regular basis. I'd&nbsp;
have dreams of being ripped apart."

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.960
McCune is no stranger to nightmares,&nbsp;
having appeared in 11 episodes of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.120
Walking Dead" as farmhand Jimmy. He has&nbsp;
also made appearances on series such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.200
"Shameless," "Homeland," and "How to Get Away&nbsp;
with Murder." In films, he's appeared opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.080
the Rock in "Snitch," and had a role in Mary&nbsp;
Harron's tragic Lifetime movie "Anna Nicole"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.160
Recently, he has also added the roles of&nbsp;
producer and assistant director to his profile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.320
having worked on the web series "Sugar&nbsp;
Pine 7," and the short film "Taipei Jesus."

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:08.080
Lisa Arlington wanted to make a&nbsp;
documentary called "The Absence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.920
of Closure," including the story of Heather&nbsp;
Donahue's brother and her boyfriend James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.080
and even "launched" a Kickstarter to help&nbsp;
her make it a reality. In actual reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.960
Lisa Arlington was played by Callie Hernandez,&nbsp;
who had a "weirdly fun" time working on the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.600
"Witch" film's memorable dirty tunnel scene.&nbsp;
In an interview with /Film, she recalled having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.840
a better time handling the close quarters&nbsp;
than the claustrophobic "camera guy" did.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.760
Hernandez's acting career didn't start&nbsp;
until her early 20s, but when it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.480
it started with a bang, landing a role that was&nbsp;
later cut in Terrence Malick's "Song to Song."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.000
That was followed by a trio of&nbsp;
parts in Robert Rodriguez projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.480
including "Machete Kills". While shooting "Blair&nbsp;
Witch" in Vancouver, she made a singing tape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:50.400
that led to her playing one of Emma Stone's&nbsp;
roommates in "La La Land." Her credits also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.880
include Ridley Scott's "Alien: Covenant,"&nbsp;
TV's "Graves" and "Under the Silver Lake."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.960
Next on her dance card is joining Jennifer&nbsp;
Lopez and Josh Duhamel for "Shotgun Wedding."

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.360
Peter is James' skeptical, childhood&nbsp;
friend in the third "Blair Witch" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.240
and is played by Tuscaloosa native Brandon&nbsp;
Scott. Scott told his hometown newspaper that

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.320
"[2016's "Blair Witch"] is more of an homage&nbsp;
to the first one, but it takes the story to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.160
the nth degree, even though it leaves a&nbsp;
lot of Easter eggs, and open questions.”

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.920
A graduate of New York University's&nbsp;
Tisch School of the Arts in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.960
Scott has racked up north of 70 credits since&nbsp;
leaving school. His voice was heard in Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.440
"Wreck-It Ralph" and "The Boss Baby" TV series,&nbsp;
and he's been seen in everything from Showtime's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.920
"Guerilla" to "This is Us" to "Dead to Me" and&nbsp;
"Goliath." In a "Blair Witch" alumni pairing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.400
Scott played "Lucky" in Joshua Leonard's&nbsp;
2018 directed feature "Dark Was the Night."

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.600
Corbin Reid was "definitely scared" when reading&nbsp;
the script for "Blair Witch," for which she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:47.760
cast as Ashley. The poor girl gets a nasty&nbsp;
infection on her foot during the journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.200
which is one of the last things&nbsp;
you'd want in the deep dark woods.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.080
In real life, Reid was able to escape the forest&nbsp;
and nab 13 episode stints on both "How to Get Away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.200
with Murder" and "Valor." In 2019, she wrote,&nbsp;
directed and executive produced the short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.760
"Blind Sight," and she is currently making waves&nbsp;
starring on the Starz series "Run The World."

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.160
The Minnesotan is an avid long-distance runner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.560
a lover of puppies, and interior design.&nbsp;
Her dream role is to play Whitney Houston.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.360
In the third "Blair Witch" film, locals Lane&nbsp;
and Talia are conspiracy theorists who found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.280
lost footage of Heather Donahue taped to&nbsp;
a tree in the woods. Their find sets the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.880
plot in motion, with the two acting&nbsp;
as guides for James and his friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:29.280
Valorie Curry, who played Talia, never saw the&nbsp;
original when she was younger, telling Vogue,

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.760
"I was terrified of E.T. as a small&nbsp;
child! I had a low threshold for fear."

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:38.320
Curry's early acting career began with 6 episodes&nbsp;
of "Veronica Mars," but she took a break soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.520
after to see if another vocation would stick ...&nbsp;
and it didn't. She returned to the craft in 2011&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:48.720
with parts on "CSI: NY," "Psych," "The Twilight&nbsp;
Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 2," and even appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.600
in blink-182's music video for their song&nbsp;
"After Midnight." Her residency on the show "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.840
Following" not only earned her a steady paycheck,&nbsp;
but it was also where she met her future husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.680
actor Sam Underwood. The two have since acted&nbsp;
together in a pair of short films, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:06.400
Edinburgh Fringe Festival, and even performed&nbsp;
music together live. Other notable works include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.200
playing Kara in the video game "Detroit: Become&nbsp;
Human," and appearing in "The Tick" TV series.

